From 89a1101700f83a2d90c09a7c4c7f060a7f4dee2e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arealjim <6187715+arealjim@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 10:51:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] runtime: go to the long snapshot wait only after late frames
 in a row

0036 waited up to max(frame period, 100 ms) for every snapshot, so a
slow application streams at its own rate instead of black. This keeps
that, but only for an application that needs it. The wait now has two
modes (SnapshotWaitPolicy.h):

- short, min(frame period, 16.7 ms), the wait before 0036, while
  frames arrive on time. A stray late frame is dropped, as it was.
- long, max(frame period, 100 ms), after three late frames in a row.
  A frame is late when it was not ready in time or took longer than
  the short wait, i.e. when the short wait would have dropped it.
- back to short after two seconds of frames on time in a row.

The gap between the two thresholds is the hysteresis: an application
near the period reaches three late in a row quickly and then does not
get two seconds on time, so it settles on the long wait instead of
flapping. A slow application loses three frames per encoder life,
about 40 ms, before it streams.

Each switch is counted and logged at info, the first and then at most
one line a second, carrying the switches it stood for; the encoder's
shutdown logs the total when there were any. 0036's drop and late
lines are unchanged.

Asked for 2026-10-27 after an 80 Hz ABAB put 0036's reprojection
about 0.4 points above rel34's in both pairs. Note that those 0036
arms logged no wait over a period, so their frames took the path this
change keeps, and the difference may be run order, not the wait.
